== FINDING: THE HARNESS REPORTS A TRANSLATION IT IS NOT USING ==
slice0-anchor-recall.mts:45  console.error(`indexed ${verses.length} WEB verses, ...`)
slice0-anchor-recall.mts:140 console.log(`... (n=${N}, WEB shingle n=3, ...)`)

'WEB' is a hardcoded LITERAL in both lines. So is 'shingle n=3', while NGRAM = 6 (line 32).
The index itself honours BIBLE_TR correctly -- proven by running three translations over the
same input and watching the index change while the label did not:

   BIBLE_TR=kjv  -> indexed 31102 WEB verses, 588029 distinct 6-shingles
   BIBLE_TR=web  -> indexed 31103 WEB verses, 559459 distinct 6-shingles
   BIBLE_TR=asv  -> indexed 31086 WEB verses, 577970 distinct 6-shingles

Three different indexes; one label, always 'WEB'; and a shingle size reported as 3 when it is 6.

WHY IT MATTERS: the KJV/WEB difference is the single largest variable in Slice 0 -- 17 points,
the finding that created gate B4 and ADR-100. An operator re-running this and reading
'indexed ... WEB verses' would conclude they had run WEB when they had run KJV. The published
Slice 0 numbers are not in doubt: they were labelled by hand in prose, not taken from this
output. What is broken is the instrument's REPORT, not its behaviour (THE_LOOP rule 3).

DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED. A label is not a parameter, regex, shingle length, window or bar, so
correcting it would not tune a number -- but it would change the file hash, and PRE-REGISTRATION
§2 rests the freeze on 'git-verified unchanged'. These scripts are also being DEMOTED to a
regression check by ADR-103. So: left byte-identical, recorded here, and every invocation in
this slice states BIBLE_TR explicitly. The vN harness built for ADR-103 reports its translation
FROM the env var, and a test asserts the reported label matches the directory actually read.

== SECOND CONFIRMATION, INCIDENTAL ==
Run over Whitefield (data/raw/archive/whitefield-works/pg68976.txt): '0 stated-text markers
-> 0 usable sermons'. That independently reproduces the B0a blocker recorded in RESULT.md --
non-Spurgeon authors state the reference FIRST, so the epigraph regex matches nothing. It is
the concrete reason ADR-103's no-epigraph metric was needed.
